@geoffkizer in this particular case:

The following will be backed by a dual-mode socket:
```C#
TcpClient client = new TcpClient();
client.Connect(host, port);
```

The following will be backed by either an IPV6 or an IPV4 socket depending on what `host` resolves to first:
```C#
TcpClient client = new TcpClient(host, port);
```

One can argue that this makes sense, since the client was created as `AddressFamily.Unknown` in the first case, and for a specific host (thus AddressFamily) in the second one (see https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/47058#discussion_r559790115), but IMHO such a semantical difference is not obvious from the two API usages, and harmonizing everything towards the same behavior would be better.

We should either prefer DualMode, or if we believe it is unreliable, we need to switch to a static `Socket.Connect(Async)` in our stack.

